In terms of behavior, the Mexican Jay is known to be highly social and gregarious, often seen in flocks. They are omnivorous, feeding on seeds, fruits, insects, and small vertebrates. They are also known for their intelligence and problem-solving abilities, as they have been observed caching food and using tools to open nuts.

The Mexican Jay can be distinguished from other similar species by its unique coloration and size. For example, the Blue Jay (Cyanocitta cristata) has a more vibrant blue coloration and a larger size, while the Scrub Jay (Aphelocoma coerulescens) has a more muted coloration and a shorter tail. Additionally, the Mexican Jay's habitat preference for shrubs and trees in arid regions further distinguishes it from other jay species.
