MLLM description: The Prairie Warbler is a small, slender bird with a compact body and short tail. Its most distinctive feature is its bright yellow underparts, which contrast sharply with its olive-green upperparts and wings. The head is also olive-green, with a pale supercilium (eyebrow) stripe that extends from the base of the bill to behind the eye. The bird has a black mask-like marking around the eyes, which is more prominent in males than females.

The Prairie Warbler's coloration is consistent across its range, although there may be slight variations in intensity of yellow and green depending on the individual and the season. The plumage of the juvenile is similar to the adult, but it lacks the black mask and has a duller overall appearance.

The size of the Prairie Warbler is relatively small, with an average length of about 5 inches (13 cm). It has a short, pointed bill and a thin, slightly curved tail. The legs and feet are dark brown or black.

Behaviorally, the Prairie Warbler is known for its active and agile movements within the understory of forests and shrublands. It is often seen foraging on the ground, probing into leaf litter and decaying wood for insects and other small invertebrates.
